    "q-71143": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71143",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int e\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int e\\,\\text{d}x & =e\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =e\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =ex+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
